WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Knowing that Claude routes on descriptions is the foundation of tool selection.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Writing a description that actually works across varied inputs, handles ambiguous cases,

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
and holds up under real-world agent traffic is the craft.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.000
That's what this lesson is about.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.000
These ideas frame everything we'll do from here related to tool use.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
A strong tool description has three required components.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.000
First is what it does, a clear purpose statement covering the tool's function and its return value.

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Second is when to use it, the explicit conditions that should trigger selection.

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Third is when not to use it, the exclusion clause that tells Claude what falls out of scope.

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Anthropic recommends at least three to four sentences per description,

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:54.000
and more when the tool is complex, because that is usually what it takes to cover all three parts well.

12
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
The third component is the one most commonly skipped,

13
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.000
and it is often the direct reason for misrouting when tools share a similar function.

14
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Leave any component out, and you have opened a routing gap that surfaces when your agent is handling real traffic.

15
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Your description should also signal what kinds of inputs the tool expects.

16
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
This doesn't mean pasting the JSON schema into the description.

17
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
It means summarizing the input intent.

18
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
If the tool takes a customer ID and a date range, say so.

19
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
If it expects a query string, say that.

20
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.000
If the input needs to be a valid enum value, name the constraint.

21
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.000
These input hints in the description help Claude form the call correctly, not just select the right tool.

22
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
There's a meaningful difference between those two outcomes.

23
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:48.000
One gets you the right tool, the other gets you a correct tool invocation with well-formed arguments.

24
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Output format is equally important and equally skipped.

25
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:58.000
When Claude selects a tool, it's implicitly asking whether calling this tool will produce what the request needs.

26
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
If the description doesn't say what the tool returns, Claude can't answer that question with confidence.

27
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Tell it whether you're returning a single object, a list, a Boolean, or a status code.

28
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Note whether the output is ready to use or requires transformation.

29
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Flag how errors surface.

30
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Does the tool return null, raise an exception, or include an error field in the response?

31
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Output framing completes the picture Claude needs to select and use a tool correctly.

32
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.664
When your tool surface grows, three strategies keep selection unambiguous, and the first two come

33
00:02:33.664 --> 00:02:35.941
straight from Anthropic's own guidance.

34
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.000
The first is consolidation.

35
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.506
Rather than a separate tool for every action, like create, review, and merge, group the related

36
00:02:44.506 --> 00:02:47.931
operations into one tool with an action parameter.

37
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Fewer, more capable tools mean less for Claude to disambiguate.

38
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.000
The second is namespacing.

39
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:01.766
When tools span multiple services, prefix each name with its service, like github underscore listprs

40
00:03:01.766 --> 00:03:03.932
or slack underscore sendmessage,

41
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.000
so the owning service is obvious from the name alone.

42
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.000
The third is explicit exclusion.

43
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.000
State what a tool doesn't handle, such as it doesn't do bulk updates or deletions.

44
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Together, these shrink the space Claude has to reason over, and make the right tool the obvious one.

45
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Let's look at what happens when two tools have overlapping, vague descriptions, versus when they're cleanly separated.

46
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
With overlap, Claude has no confident basis for selection.

47
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.000
It might call either tool, and the routing is essentially unpredictable, across varied input phrasings.

48
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.173
With clean separation of domain anchors, trigger conditions, and exclusion clauses, Claude selects

49
00:03:44.173 --> 00:03:45.937
the right tool consistently.

50
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.000
The descriptions did the disambiguation work up front, so Claude doesn't have to guess at runtime.

51
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.000
That's the standard you're aiming for.

52
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.118
You want descriptions so precise and well-bounded that correct selection becomes the reliable

53
00:04:00.118 --> 00:04:03.934
outcome, regardless of how the user phrases their request.

54
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Writing a good description gets you to step one.

55
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:13.000
Confirming it routes correctly across the real input distribution gets you to step two.

56
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.000
And there are three tests worth running before you ship.

57
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.406
A positive test sends a request the tool should handle, and confirms both correct selection and

58
00:04:21.406 --> 00:04:22.943
correct argument formation.

59
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.986
A negative test sends a request the tool shouldn't handle, and confirms Claude routes to an

60
00:04:28.986 --> 00:04:32.934
alternative, or asks for clarification rather than guessing.

61
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:39.816
A disambiguation test sends a request that plausibly matches more than one tool, and confirms the

62
00:04:39.816 --> 00:04:45.929
right one wins on description specificity, not position in the list or name similarity.

63
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Run all three before deploying any toolset, and you get evidence-based confidence in routing reliability.

64
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.000
In production, this can go wrong quietly.

65
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:01.433
A toolset that routes perfectly with a handful of tools starts picking the wrong one once the

66
00:05:01.433 --> 00:05:05.930
catalog grows and users phrase requests that you never predicted.

67
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Purpose, trigger conditions, and exclusions keep those boundaries sharp.

68
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:20.000
And the positive, negative, and disambiguation tests are how you prove they hold before real traffic finds a gap.

69
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.000
That wraps up this one. I'll see you in the next lesson.

